<ArticleLayout
  title="How do you learn to change? An insight from sorrow"
  subtitle=""
  description="There's a pattern I noticed in myself relatively late. Late enough that it had already caused damage."
  image="/images/placeholder.jpg"
  date="2024-05-15"
  readTime="X min read"
  tags={['Personal Reflection', 'Sorrow', 'Change', 'Relationships', 'Colombia', 'Sweden']}
  draft={true}
>
  <p>There's a pattern I noticed in myself relatively late. Late enough that it had already caused damage.</p>

  <p>Growing up in Colombia, loss was structural. Not metaphorical. People died. Families displaced. Relatives who disappeared from one chapter to the next without clean endings. You don't process that as a child. You absorb it as grammar. A set of rules about how closeness works, what it costs, and what it tends to become. Without realizing it, I carried that grammar into every relationship that mattered. I didn't reproduce the specific losses. I reproduced the shape of them.</p>

  <p>Colombia was specific. The collective was the unit. Family, neighborhood, extended belonging. For me that also meant the scouting movement, years of it, a structured community with explicit values, roles, service as identity. You knew who you were partly because you knew where you stood in relation to others. Identity held in common before it's held individually. I didn't have the vocabulary for it at the time. But that's what was happening: a developmental stage where the collective does the holding, before the harder work of individuation begins.</p>

  <p>When I moved to Sweden for university, the container broke. That's not a complaint. It needed to. Swedish culture operates on a strong assumption of individual autonomy. You are responsible for yourself. Others are responsible for themselves. There's dignity in that, and also a kind of loneliness that doesn't announce itself. The first real separation. You step outside the collective that defined you and have to construct your own terms. I didn't do it gracefully. Nobody really does.</p>

  <p>At K9, the design school in Gothenburg, something different opened. A chosen collective. Not the one I was born into, not the one I joined as a child with its codes and ceremonies, but one I arrived at through shared work and shared sensibility. The belonging felt different because it was elective. We had a Slack channel called 'holdmyhair,' a reference to holding someone's hair back while they're sick. When you need a hand and you can't help back. That's what showing up looked like. No scaffolding of institution or family obligation. Just the decision to be there.</p>

  <p>What I didn't see clearly enough then was that the loss template was still running underneath. The grammar from Colombia, from early experiences of people and places that ended without resolution. Attachment that already anticipated its own failure. I recreated those dynamics in relationships. Not consciously, not dramatically, but structurally. The pattern was there.</p>

  <p>When I finally saw it, it didn't arrive as revelation. More like recognizing a shape you've been moving around for years and suddenly having a name for it. What made the recognition real, not just intellectual, was causing pain to someone I loved through exactly these mechanisms. The loss template wasn't something I carried privately. It played out on people. That's when it became impossible to theorize around.</p>

  <p>There's something in the language worth pausing on. "I am sorry" comes from sorrow. In Swedish, "jag är ledsen" literally means "I am sad." Both languages embed grief into the act of apologizing. I didn't encounter this as a linguistic curiosity. I lived it first and then the etymology made sense. Heartbreak, in this case, was not mine received. It was mine caused. That's a different kind of sorrow. Heavier, more clarifying.</p>

  <p>What that sorrow asked of me was to stay inside it. Not perform it for others. Not rush toward resolution to relieve the discomfort. Not narrate or analyze my way out of it. Just hold it. Most emotional movement is defensive. We replace one feeling with another before the first one has done its work. Holding is the refusal to do that.</p>

  <p>I'm not past the pattern. I still recognize it when it activates. What's different is that I can see it now, name it, and sometimes choose differently. Sometimes not. Understanding your own patterns is not freedom from them. It's more like learning the terrain. You still have to walk it.</p>

  <p>The question has shifted over the years. Less "what does this place offer?" and more "what does this place ask of me?" A small difference in grammar. Maybe the most durable thing sorrow taught.</p>
